drills.explanation:
The planted bug is the `await` inside the `foreach`. Each fetch starts only after the previous one finished, so the calls run one at a time. Order and cancellation still work, so those tests pass, but "the fetches run concurrently" sees a peak of 1 where it expects 4. Start every fetch first, then await them together with `Task.WhenAll`, which also returns the prices in input order:

```csharp
var tasks = symbols.Select(s => fetch(s, ct)).ToList();
var prices = await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
return symbols.Select((s, i) => new Quote(s, prices[i])).ToList();
```

With 20 symbols at 200 ms each, that is the difference between about 4 seconds and about 200 ms.
